cricket always feels better when the  stumps are in the middle the sun is out  and play is underway  but the racism scandal is still playing  out in yorkshire with the county cricket  club itself now charged with bringing  the game into disrepute the allegations  go back to 2004. felt isolated  humiliated at times  former player azeem rafiq laid out to  mps how racism had blighted his and so  many other careers  cricket had to act  rafiq who could still be disciplined  himself over his own anti-semitism  welcomed the charges he said this has  been another grueling but unfortunately  necessary process it's been a long two  years since i went public about my  experiences but i hope this all means  that no young player ever goes through  such pain and alienation again  young players here in bradford and in  all corners of the uk  need a more equal and accessible sport  certainly at grassroots levels we you  know we've actually yet to see any  changes any positive changes have  actually happened some individuals and  some organizations have done quite well  out of the changes that happen but again  people working at the grassroots levels  we're still waiting for something to  happen  at headingley it's been a painful  process lord patel came in 16 members of  staff were sacked some of whom have now  won their cases of unfair dismissal and  now all-rounder david willey has quit  saying the club is too fixated on  repairing its reputation  the new sponsors here are called clean  slate  next week they'll be hosting  international test cricket here once  again a sign that significant progress  has been made but racism and archaic  attitudes don't just disappear overnight  because an organization wants them to  this is a well-supported proud  institution and that brings a range of  thinking through the gates if you go on  to the terraces and in the the stands  are heading me now  yeah you still hear  a denial right there's a very strong  denial that i am institutionally raci